NAME
       renice - change the nice value of running processes

SYNOPSIS
       renice [-n] PRIORITY [-p] PID...
       renice [-n] PRIORITY -u USER...
       renice [-n] PRIORITY -g PGRP...

DESCRIPTION
       renice sets the nice value (-20 to 19) of processes that are already
       running. Unlike nice, the number is the new value itself, not an
       amount to add. For each process it prints the old and the new value.

       A normal user can only renice their own processes, and only to a
       higher (nicer) value than they have. Root can set any value. The
       process keeps running; only its share of the CPU changes.

       The usual steps: find the process (top, sorted by CPU, or ps aux
       --sort=-%cpu, or pgrep), then renice it by its PID.

OPTIONS
       -n, --priority N
              The new nice value. The -n may be left out: "renice 10 1234".

       -p, --pid
              The following arguments are process IDs (the default).

       -u, --user
              The following arguments are user names or UIDs: renice all
              their processes.

       -g, --pgrp
              The following arguments are process group IDs.

EXAMPLES
       Give process 2481 the nice value 10:

           renice -n 10 -p 2481

       Find the process using the most CPU and make it nicer:

           ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head -3
           renice -n 15 -p 3172

       Make every process of the user student nicer:

           renice -n 5 -u student

       Change the nice value of every process named dd, then check:

           renice -n 19 $(pgrep dd)
           ps -o pid,ni,comm -p $(pgrep dd)

       In top, the r key does the same: type the PID, then the value.

SEE ALSO
       nice(1), ps(1), top(1), pgrep(1)
